# {{ project_name }}

Standalone CloudFormation infrastructure with private versioned S3 storage. Initialization and all engineering checks are offline and never deploy resources. Python 3.12+ runs the portable repository scripts; there is no Python application package.

Run `python scripts/project.py bootstrap` to install missing cfn-lint 1.57.2 in `.tools/.venv` and Guard 3.2.1 in `.tools/bin`. Requires uv and network access for initial installation. Guard uses official release archives with pinned SHA-256 checksums. Windows, Linux and macOS x86_64/ARM64 are supported. Existing local or PATH tools are reused. No global installation or shell PATH change is performed. Subsequent checks find local tools automatically and remain offline.

Bootstrap prepares tools, syncs and checks the project. Afterwards run `python scripts/project.py verify`. Both cfn-lint and cfn-guard must be available. These validate syntax, properties, references and selected security policies; they do not prove complete cloud security or successful deployment. Use `python scripts/project.py work --help`, `route --help`, `skill --help`, `checkpoint --help`, and `resume --help` for engineering workflows.

`AGENTS.md` is canonical; `CLAUDE.md` imports it. Canonical skills are `.agents/skills`; sync creates thin Claude views. `.agentready/` is optional maintenance metadata: detach preserves infrastructure, checks, CI, work records and instructions. Deployment needs separate explicit authorization and account-aware review.

## Repository organization

`stacks/<domain>/` owns resources, inputs and outputs; `stacks/catalog.conf` defines stage order and environment enablement; `parameters/` stores synthetic environment parameter values; `policies/` owns Guard security constraints. `scripts/` contains standard-library governance commands. `.agents/skills/` is canonical; Claude adapters point there. Work records live in `docs/work/`; verification receipts in `docs/verification/` and checkpoints in `docs/work/checkpoints/`.

## Add an infrastructure component

Search the map and existing templates. Add a focused `stacks/<domain>/<component>.yaml`, avoiding fixed physical names, real account IDs and secrets. Describe parameter/output contracts, ownership and retention in `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`. Add positive and relevant negative fixtures, then run `python scripts/project.py sync` and `python scripts/project.py verify`. Extend fixture_check.py explicitly when adding a new failure expectation.

## Work lifecycle and evidence

Create work with `python scripts/project.py work new --type FEATURE "Title"`; define normative scope and acceptance before moving BACKLOG to READY and IN_PROGRESS. Route using `python scripts/project.py route FEATURE --work-status READY`. Run `python scripts/project.py verify --work W001` before DONE and complete the implementation record. A successful receipt is required; prose claiming tests passed is insufficient. Existing DONE items predating receipts remain legacy records; receipts are required for newly created work. DOCS verification records governance and map checks without infrastructure tools. Use `checkpoint` and `resume` commands to preserve and validate interrupted context.

## Security scope

The static baseline checks S3 encryption presence, public access blocking, versioning, retention, plaintext-transport deny presence, and unrestricted ingress. For bucket templates the TLS check requires at least one suitable deny statement; explicitly review policy association and Resource coverage for every bucket. IAM least privilege, encryption details, network intent and account-specific controls require review. Static checks do not prove deployment success.

Deployment is a separate manual action requiring explicit authorization, credentials, target-account review and change-set review. None of bootstrap, check, verify or sync deploys resources.

## Catalog and optional deployment

The initial storage stack is registered but disabled (`-`). Add only purposeful domains,
register templates in lexical stage order, and use stable parameter/output names. Both
`.yaml` and `.yml` work. Run `bash scripts/deploy.sh catalog` offline.

Read [the engineering contract](docs/infrastructure/ENGINEERING.md) for SSM inputs,
explicit branch/account/region mapping, OIDC provider/bootstrap/DeploymentRole/ExecutionRole
separation, optional pre-push hooks and Bitbucket custom steps. `deployment/environments.conf`
is deliberately unconfigured. Default pipelines only validate; no AWS writes run on pushes.
The optional Bash/AWS CLI runner requires separate plan and exact-change-set execution
authorization and rejects removal/replacement risk. It has no Python deployment dependencies.
